{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792745272,"nonce":"QByLNm6Tqiw5yQqTwx7jFxuHNjw=","title":"Retain abstract conversion rules across files and aliases","message":"LSP reports invalid mismatches for declared abstract conversions, including a typedef of an abstract with from/to clauses and cross-module @:from/@:to methods. A minimal session produced six false mismatches for Int -\u003e alias -\u003e Int and String -\u003e abstract -\u003e String in returns and local declarations.\n\nConversion rules are currently held only in AstLowering's per-file map. The separate type checking phase cannot consult them, later files lose them, and return/local initializer paths do not consistently apply conversion methods. The fix shares declared conversions in the type graph, restores BLADE conversion metadata, follows typedefs, substitutes generic arguments, and handles conversion methods in returns/initializers. Negative tests must still reject an abstract without a matching declared conversion and incorrect generic instantiations.\n\nThe reported BcEncoder screenshot additionally suggests a short-name identity collision (haxe.io.Bytes versus hl.Bytes). A small two-package collision fixture currently resolves correctly; the actual BcEncoder file is being isolated from RayDE initialization macros to reproduce this independently.","files":null}]}